WEBVTT

1
00:00:44.080 --> 00:00:46.355
We were a real business.

2
00:00:46.520 --> 00:00:48.909
That's what
you got to understand.

3
00:00:51.240 --> 00:00:53.390
We were goddamn industry...

4
00:00:54.840 --> 00:00:57.673
...the kind that's the backbone
of this country.

5
00:00:59.640 --> 00:01:00.755
Don't take my word for it.

6
00:01:00.880 --> 00:01:02.456
Go in the parking lot
and look on the license

7
00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:04.789
of that brand-new
Mercedes 450 of yours.

8
00:01:06.040 --> 00:01:08.554
Hey. Are you seeing this?

9
00:01:08.680 --> 00:01:09.999
That's Furnace Creek.

10
00:01:10.120 --> 00:01:11.656
This, uh, picture's
over 100 years old.

11
00:01:11.680 --> 00:01:12.954
Hmm.

12
00:01:13.080 --> 00:01:14.936
And you see this
handsome devil right here?

13
00:01:14.960 --> 00:01:16.552
Uh-huh.

14
00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:18.233
He's a Wells.

15
00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:22.148
Okay, so, um, I know
you're a mining company,

16
00:01:22.280 --> 00:01:25.078
- Mm-hmm.
- but I pictured...

17
00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:26.880
Well, I don't know
what I pictured exactly.

18
00:01:26.920 --> 00:01:28.592
I guess little... little carts

19
00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:30.836
going down little...
little train tracks.

20
00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:32.800
- Right.
- I mean, what is it that you do here?

21
00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:34.711
Wh-what we do
is we look for the metals

22
00:01:34.840 --> 00:01:36.034
and the minerals,
all right?

23
00:01:36.160 --> 00:01:37.912
There's exploration
over there,

24
00:01:38.040 --> 00:01:41.271
there's engineering
and there's production, right?

25
00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:43.755
We're prospectors.

26
00:01:44.880 --> 00:01:46.518
Not yet?

27
00:01:46.640 --> 00:01:48.039
- Okay, how about this?
- Yeah...

28
00:01:48.160 --> 00:01:51.675
Um... so let's say that this...

29
00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:53.068
is the Earth.

30
00:01:53.200 --> 00:01:54.200
My handbag?

31
00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:55.912
Your handbag is Mother Earth

32
00:01:56.040 --> 00:01:57.598
with all of her
deep-filled mysteries

33
00:01:57.760 --> 00:01:59.936
and goodies on the inside that
she does not want to give up.

34
00:01:59.960 --> 00:02:01.678
Me, I'm man,

35
00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:03.870
and I'm damn curious about
what she's got inside.

36
00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:07.037
So, I send down what's
called an exploratory drill.

37
00:02:07.160 --> 00:02:10.038
I go down inside
and dig around,

38
00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:11.718
come across something.

39
00:02:11.840 --> 00:02:14.195
I pull it out. It's nickel.

40
00:02:14.360 --> 00:02:17.750
Now, nickel's actually
75% copper and 25% nickel,

41
00:02:17.880 --> 00:02:19.176
but there's a whole lot
of nickel in the world,

42
00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:21.634
and the world needs nickels.

43
00:02:21.760 --> 00:02:23.830
I'm back down there getting
my second core sample,

44
00:02:23.960 --> 00:02:26.269
and I happen to pull up, say...

45
00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:27.879
silver.

46
00:02:28.040 --> 00:02:29.696
Now, the thing is, when you
find a little bit of this,

47
00:02:29.720 --> 00:02:31.416
that means there could be
a whole hell of a lot of it.

48
00:02:31.440 --> 00:02:32.953
So we start raising money.

49
00:02:33.080 --> 00:02:35.469
We raise enough money
to open a mine

50
00:02:35.600 --> 00:02:37.272
like the one in that picture
right here.

51
00:02:37.400 --> 00:02:40.551
Then...

52
00:02:40.720 --> 00:02:42.756
we dig it all up.

53
00:02:42.880 --> 00:02:47.158
Now, sometimes...
not that often, but sometimes...

54
00:02:47.280 --> 00:02:50.272
the prospecting gods are having
a party at the Pearly Gates,

55
00:02:50.440 --> 00:02:53.318
and we get real, real,
really lucky.

56
00:02:53.480 --> 00:02:55.152
And we dig down in there,

57
00:02:55.320 --> 00:02:58.153
then we find
a little metal called...

58
00:02:58.320 --> 00:03:00.311
gold.

59
00:03:04.320 --> 00:03:06.356
- Yes.
- Kenny.

60
00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:07.913
You like it?

61
00:03:08.080 --> 00:03:09.752
I love you.

62
00:03:13.360 --> 00:03:16.158
I love you.

63
00:03:16.280 --> 00:03:18.157
Old man wants to see you.

64
00:03:19.680 --> 00:03:21.352
Afternoon, Marianne.

65
00:03:21.480 --> 00:03:23.336
Hey, Pop.
Heard you want to see me.

66
00:03:23.360 --> 00:03:24.554
Yeah, Kenny.

67
00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:27.109
Get your ass in here,
would you?

68
00:03:27.240 --> 00:03:28.593
Would you, uh, run through this

69
00:03:28.720 --> 00:03:30.597
Pershing County strategy
again for me?

70
00:03:30.720 --> 00:03:32.870
Right, um...

71
00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:35.355
It's about this southern part
of this Newmont property,

72
00:03:35.480 --> 00:03:36.754
right here.

73
00:03:36.880 --> 00:03:39.189
Now, Newmont just started
drilling 140 meters

74
00:03:39.360 --> 00:03:41.096
and they're finding
a whole lot of magnesite.

75
00:03:41.120 --> 00:03:43.873
If you remember, we still own
the option on Relief Canyon,

76
00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:46.036
which is next door.
We're not finding squat, why?

77
00:03:46.200 --> 00:03:47.758
We're not drilling
deep enough.

78
00:03:47.880 --> 00:03:49.598
Let's get back in there.
Drill deeper.

79
00:03:49.720 --> 00:03:52.712
Go to 140 if we need to,
and we got a damn good chance

80
00:03:52.840 --> 00:03:54.671
of getting Relief
back in production.

81
00:03:54.800 --> 00:03:56.677
What about Jerritt Canyon?

82
00:03:56.800 --> 00:03:58.631
Well, that's your baby.

83
00:03:58.800 --> 00:04:01.030
You know a hell of a lot
more about it than I do.

84
00:04:01.160 --> 00:04:02.752
But the assays look promising.

85
00:04:02.880 --> 00:04:04.472
Could be fucking huge.

86
00:04:09.480 --> 00:04:11.357
I want you to run point
on this.

87
00:04:11.520 --> 00:04:13.670
All in.

88
00:04:13.800 --> 00:04:15.870
Scale it up.

89
00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:17.831
Make it yours.

90
00:04:17.960 --> 00:04:20.428
Yes, sir.

91
00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:25.035
Clive Coleman's on board.

92
00:04:25.200 --> 00:04:26.713
Those boys will ride with you.

93
00:04:34.200 --> 00:04:36.430
God, we work hard
in this business.

94
00:04:36.560 --> 00:04:38.391
Sometimes for nothing.

95
00:04:40.360 --> 00:04:42.555
I wake up every morning.
I tell myself,

96
00:04:42.680 --> 00:04:45.399
"I don't have to do this.

97
00:04:45.520 --> 00:04:47.351
I get to do this."

98
00:04:49.840 --> 00:04:53.389
And then it's just blue skies.

99
00:05:02.280 --> 00:05:05.033
Blue skies, baby.

100
00:05:08.920 --> 00:05:11.229
My old man...

101
00:05:11.360 --> 00:05:13.316
a legend.

102
00:05:14.760 --> 00:05:18.230
That was the last time
I saw him alive.

103
00:05:51.680 --> 00:05:53.238
A little eye-opener, Kenny?

104
00:05:53.360 --> 00:05:55.590
God bless you, Roy.

105
00:05:55.720 --> 00:05:58.632
Here we are.

106
00:05:58.760 --> 00:06:01.115
- Boys, to the mother lode.
- Mother lode.

107
00:06:01.240 --> 00:06:03.231
Today be the day.

108
00:06:03.360 --> 00:06:05.316
The geological reports on this

109
00:06:05.440 --> 00:06:06.475
are highly encouraging.

110
00:06:06.600 --> 00:06:09.068
We are taking a very
aggressive approach.

111
00:06:09.200 --> 00:06:10.200
Prospectus?
Of course.

112
00:06:10.280 --> 00:06:12.056
I can have my secretary
send you one right out.

113
00:06:12.080 --> 00:06:14.230
But I got to tell you,
this offering,

114
00:06:14.360 --> 00:06:16.032
it's already over-subscribed.

115
00:06:16.160 --> 00:06:18.096
You know, we can always try
and get you in on the next one.

116
00:06:18.120 --> 00:06:20.714
No, no.
Yea, I understand.

117
00:06:21.880 --> 00:06:23.074
Shit.

118
00:06:23.200 --> 00:06:25.953
Watching the time?
It's 11:15, Kenny.

119
00:06:26.080 --> 00:06:28.435
Oh, Christ.

120
00:06:30.360 --> 00:06:32.237
Where would I be without you?

121
00:06:39.560 --> 00:06:41.596
Mr. Wells?

122
00:06:41.720 --> 00:06:43.438
- Yes.
- Lloyd Stanton.

123
00:06:43.560 --> 00:06:45.232
- Hello, Lloyd. Kenny Wells.
- Hi.

124
00:06:45.360 --> 00:06:46.816
This is my colleague,
Henry Andrews.

125
00:06:46.840 --> 00:06:47.960
- Pleased to meet you.
- Hey.

126
00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:50.230
- Come on back.
- Absolutely.

127
00:06:50.360 --> 00:06:52.715
Thank you.

128
00:06:56.000 --> 00:06:58.958
So, what can we do for ya?

129
00:06:59.080 --> 00:07:00.957
Well, why don't we wait
for Clive?

130
00:07:01.080 --> 00:07:03.310
Oh, uh, Mr. Coleman
got tied up in a meeting.

131
00:07:03.480 --> 00:07:04.833
No offense, boys,

132
00:07:04.960 --> 00:07:07.076
but I'm supposed to be meeting
with Clive.

133
00:07:07.200 --> 00:07:08.997
None taken, but if you were
supposed to be

134
00:07:09.120 --> 00:07:11.190
meeting with Clive, you
would be meeting with Clive.

135
00:07:14.240 --> 00:07:16.117
All right.
Here is what we have.

136
00:07:16.240 --> 00:07:19.232
We are developing some
very exciting properties

137
00:07:19.360 --> 00:07:22.113
that are spot-on
for your investor profile.

138
00:07:22.240 --> 00:07:26.950
Low buy-in
with a sizable upside.

139
00:07:27.080 --> 00:07:28.496
- Mm-hmm.
- And the beautiful part is

140
00:07:28.520 --> 00:07:32.229
they are all only 12 to 18
months and cash positive.

141
00:07:32.360 --> 00:07:33.554
- Manitoba?
- Yeah.

142
00:07:33.720 --> 00:07:36.473
We picked up an option
on a skip claim at an auction.

143
00:07:36.640 --> 00:07:38.153
Right, but it's
a 90-day option.

144
00:07:38.280 --> 00:07:39.599
Yes, the window is narrow,

145
00:07:39.720 --> 00:07:41.040
but if you have a look
at the geo,

146
00:07:41.120 --> 00:07:42.496
you'll see a very promising
shale formation

147
00:07:42.520 --> 00:07:44.829
that hasn't been tapped yet.

148
00:07:47.640 --> 00:07:49.790
Mr. Wells,
Washoe has a practical value

149
00:07:49.920 --> 00:07:51.114
hovering just above zero.

150
00:07:51.280 --> 00:07:53.032
Your debt load is untenable,

151
00:07:53.200 --> 00:07:55.953
and you bring us, uh, raw land
with no infrastructure,

152
00:07:56.120 --> 00:07:58.350
- no fungible assets.
- I mean,

153
00:07:58.480 --> 00:08:00.176
you can't possibly expect us
to underwrite this.

154
00:08:00.200 --> 00:08:02.430
Yeah. I'm talking about
a small offering here.

155
00:08:02.560 --> 00:08:04.160
As I said in the beginning,
a low buy-in.

156
00:08:04.200 --> 00:08:06.350
Now, if you'll take
a second look at the geo,

157
00:08:06.480 --> 00:08:08.516
you will see what I see.

158
00:08:08.640 --> 00:08:11.029
And that's money.

159
00:08:13.160 --> 00:08:15.037
Not our money.

160
00:08:42.280 --> 00:08:43.793
See, the... the thing is

161
00:08:43.960 --> 00:08:46.155
that the whole economy
had turned,

162
00:08:46.280 --> 00:08:48.589
particularly for commodities.

163
00:08:51.240 --> 00:08:52.912
Close of the day,

164
00:08:53.040 --> 00:08:55.395
Washoe was trading
at four cents a share

165
00:08:55.520 --> 00:08:57.670
if it was trading at all.

166
00:08:57.800 --> 00:08:59.916
Washoe Mining. There's this
great little land lease.

167
00:09:00.040 --> 00:09:03.396
'Cause this is a once
in a lifetime opportunity.

168
00:09:03.560 --> 00:09:05.391
Right. Washoe. Washoe Mining.

169
00:09:05.560 --> 00:09:07.994
You know, carrying the...
carrying on the business.

170
00:09:08.120 --> 00:09:10.554
You know, there's still
minerals out there.

171
00:09:10.680 --> 00:09:11.920
We just got to find them,
right?

172
00:09:11.960 --> 00:09:13.016
Yeah, all right, all right.

173
00:09:13.040 --> 00:09:14.520
Thank... thank you
for your time. Bye.

174
00:09:17.400 --> 00:09:18.594
I could...

175
00:09:18.720 --> 00:09:20.233
12 to 18 months...

176
00:09:20.360 --> 00:09:22.271
cash positive.

177
00:09:22.400 --> 00:09:24.789
A deal...

178
00:09:24.920 --> 00:09:26.035
Goddamn, I am calling you.

179
00:09:26.160 --> 00:09:27.639
Fuck whatever time it is.

180
00:09:29.800 --> 00:09:32.678
Washoe Mining,

181
00:09:32.840 --> 00:09:34.592
the company that my grandfather

182
00:09:34.760 --> 00:09:37.957
literally scratched out of
the side of a Nevada mountain,

183
00:09:38.080 --> 00:09:42.153
the company that my father
made into a real player,

184
00:09:42.280 --> 00:09:45.317
was almost gone.

185
00:09:45.440 --> 00:09:47.795
Almost.

186
00:09:51.880 --> 00:09:55.156
I'd already, uh, lost my house,

187
00:09:55.320 --> 00:09:58.392
and I was, uh...
I was living with Kay.

188
00:09:58.520 --> 00:10:02.035
And, uh, we-we were about
to lose that, too.

189
00:10:12.720 --> 00:10:15.678
I had a half gallon
of Seagram's in me.

190
00:10:15.840 --> 00:10:18.434
Shit, most people would have
been dead, but not me. No.

191
00:10:20.440 --> 00:10:22.317
Instead...

192
00:10:22.440 --> 00:10:24.954
I had a dream.

193
00:10:28.480 --> 00:10:31.438
I mean, I mean,
I mean, literally, I...

194
00:10:31.560 --> 00:10:34.393
I had a dream.

195
00:10:40.760 --> 00:10:44.196
Indonesia.

196
00:10:44.320 --> 00:10:46.117
Gold.

197
00:10:49.000 --> 00:10:52.595
Now, I'd been to Indonesia
five or six years earlier.

198
00:10:52.720 --> 00:10:54.551
I still had a little money
in my pocket.

199
00:10:54.680 --> 00:10:56.318
You know,
Indonesia was booming,

200
00:10:56.440 --> 00:10:58.795
and I was looking
for a way to get in.

201
00:11:03.200 --> 00:11:04.872
Back then, if you wanted

202
00:11:05.000 --> 00:11:06.576
to put a hole in the ground
in Indonesia,

203
00:11:06.600 --> 00:11:09.717
you wanted Mike Acosta
telling you where to dig.

204
00:11:09.840 --> 00:11:12.195
He's what they called
a River Walker.

205
00:11:12.320 --> 00:11:13.992
Yeah, a real hands-on
geologist.

206
00:11:14.120 --> 00:11:15.120
The real deal.

207
00:11:17.040 --> 00:11:18.314
Oxford College, MIT.

208
00:11:18.440 --> 00:11:19.714
His mom was Venezuelan.

209
00:11:19.840 --> 00:11:22.070
His dad was English.

210
00:11:24.160 --> 00:11:25.480
Now, you see,
he'd just discovered

211
00:11:25.560 --> 00:11:28.154
the largest copper strike
in Southeast Asia,

212
00:11:28.280 --> 00:11:32.512
and everybody wanted
a piece of him, including me.

213
00:11:32.640 --> 00:11:35.313
You got the Nazca Plate,
the Pacific Plate.

214
00:11:35.440 --> 00:11:37.715
Juan de Fuca,
North American, South American.

215
00:11:37.880 --> 00:11:39.598
Aleutian, Marianas, Tonga.

216
00:11:39.760 --> 00:11:42.479
The plates rubbing
and grinding up on each other.

217
00:11:42.600 --> 00:11:44.272
Six trillion kilobytes
of pressure.

218
00:11:44.400 --> 00:11:48.154
10,000 degrees Celsius
kicking up geothermic hot spots

219
00:11:48.280 --> 00:11:50.157
all along the Pacific rim.

220
00:11:50.280 --> 00:11:54.398
He called it the, uh,
the Ring of Fire theory.

221
00:11:54.520 --> 00:11:56.511
And he definitely had
everyone's attention.

222
00:11:56.640 --> 00:11:59.154
That's how I found the copper.

223
00:11:59.320 --> 00:12:02.596
And that's why
I'll find the gold.

224
00:12:17.520 --> 00:12:18.999
You're up early.

225
00:12:19.120 --> 00:12:20.758
Hey.

226
00:12:23.000 --> 00:12:24.558
You want to talk
about yesterday.

227
00:12:24.720 --> 00:12:27.837
No. I gotta go right now, babe.
I've gotta plan. Gotta plan.

228
00:12:27.960 --> 00:12:30.190
- Oh, wait, Kenny, Kenny, listen.
- Hmm?

229
00:12:30.320 --> 00:12:32.197
Carl had an idea.

230
00:12:32.320 --> 00:12:34.595
- American Home Carl?
- Uh-huh.

231
00:12:34.720 --> 00:12:37.280
He said he could, uh,
get you on for a while,

232
00:12:37.440 --> 00:12:39.136
- if you wanted.
- Right, right, right, right.

233
00:12:39.160 --> 00:12:41.913
Yeah, I don't think that's
going to be necessary, babe.

234
00:12:42.040 --> 00:12:43.678
Yeah, but it'd
just be temporary

235
00:12:43.800 --> 00:12:45.392
till the market turns.

236
00:12:45.520 --> 00:12:48.637
We could fool around
in the warehouse.

237
00:12:48.760 --> 00:12:50.113
You know, I tell you...

238
00:12:51.600 --> 00:12:53.216
When I get done with this trip,
all right?

239
00:12:53.240 --> 00:12:54.480
Shouldn't take
more than a week.

240
00:12:54.560 --> 00:12:56.000
- All right.
- What? Where you going?

241
00:12:56.080 --> 00:12:58.389
- If it doesn't work out, I'll give Carl a call.
- Baby...

242
00:12:58.520 --> 00:13:00.216
- I'll call you from the airport.
- Wait. Wait, wait.

243
00:13:00.240 --> 00:13:01.559
Kenny!

244
00:13:01.680 --> 00:13:05.070
It was like
I was being called, you know?

245
00:13:06.840 --> 00:13:09.229
It was the gold calling.

246
00:13:09.360 --> 00:13:11.112
That may sound crazy to you,

247
00:13:11.240 --> 00:13:13.151
but if you...
if you knew that feeling,

248
00:13:13.280 --> 00:13:15.396
well, you'd know.

249
00:13:49.320 --> 00:13:51.197
Kenny Wells.
I'm down here in the, uh,

250
00:13:51.320 --> 00:13:52.760
the lobby of the, uh,
Jakarta Palace.

251
00:13:52.840 --> 00:13:54.432
Uh... Yeah, listen,

252
00:13:54.600 --> 00:13:56.079
we must've got
the times mixed up.

253
00:13:56.200 --> 00:13:58.111
Um... Let me know.
I'm down here.

254
00:13:58.240 --> 00:14:01.357
I'm in the brown suit,
I'm on the green couch.

255
00:14:01.480 --> 00:14:02.720
I'm the good-looking guy, okay?

256
00:14:02.800 --> 00:14:04.256
All right.
We'll make it happen, cap'n.

257
00:14:04.280 --> 00:14:05.474
All right.

258
00:14:10.360 --> 00:14:11.588
Wells?

259
00:14:11.720 --> 00:14:13.517
Mike.

260
00:14:16.480 --> 00:14:18.869
Good to see you again.

261
00:14:19.040 --> 00:14:22.237
I know a local place,
a bit more color.

262
00:14:22.360 --> 00:14:24.476
What, they're not pouring here?

263
00:14:25.800 --> 00:14:27.791
Good.

264
00:14:27.920 --> 00:14:30.798
So,

265
00:14:30.920 --> 00:14:33.514
what are we talking about
here, Wells?

266
00:14:35.440 --> 00:14:36.998
Ring of Fire, Mike.

267
00:14:37.120 --> 00:14:38.599
Ring of Fire.

268
00:14:38.720 --> 00:14:41.678
You're playing
my greatest hits.

269
00:14:41.800 --> 00:14:44.030
Fire's gone out of that one.

270
00:14:44.160 --> 00:14:45.912
Well, what are you
talking about?

271
00:14:46.040 --> 00:14:49.874
It's the opinion of my
fellow esteemed geologists,

272
00:14:50.000 --> 00:14:52.434
that the whole thing...
I'm quoting here...

273
00:14:52.560 --> 00:14:54.596
is a crock of shit.

274
00:14:54.720 --> 00:14:56.756
Well, what the hell, you know?

275
00:14:56.880 --> 00:14:58.632
You called it wrong.

276
00:14:59.840 --> 00:15:01.717
What are you going to do?

277
00:15:01.840 --> 00:15:04.513
There's no right or wrong
in this business.

278
00:15:04.640 --> 00:15:06.915
There's only hits and misses.

279
00:15:07.040 --> 00:15:09.634
There's gold here.

280
00:15:15.760 --> 00:15:18.354
I'm glad you said that, Mike.

281
00:15:18.520 --> 00:15:20.795
I truly am, 'cause I...

282
00:15:22.440 --> 00:15:23.998
...believe you.

283
00:15:24.120 --> 00:15:26.395
I hate to dash your hopes,
but don't you think

284
00:15:26.520 --> 00:15:28.576
others have come along
before you with the same thought

285
00:15:28.600 --> 00:15:32.513
and, from the looks of it,
deeper pockets?

286
00:15:32.640 --> 00:15:35.313
Ring of Fire is real, Mike.

287
00:15:35.440 --> 00:15:36.793
I knew it
the first time I heard.

288
00:15:36.920 --> 00:15:38.600
It struck me like
a fucking lightning bolt,

289
00:15:38.720 --> 00:15:40.312
and I never forgot it.

290
00:15:40.440 --> 00:15:42.476
Go ahead, laugh.
I get that all the time.

291
00:15:42.600 --> 00:15:45.068
But hear this.

292
00:15:45.200 --> 00:15:47.395
I don't just believe.

293
00:15:47.520 --> 00:15:48.953
I know.

294
00:15:49.080 --> 00:15:51.150
- No, you don't.
- I've seen it.

295
00:15:51.280 --> 00:15:54.397
You don't understand.

296
00:15:54.520 --> 00:15:57.717
- No one will back me.
- I will.

297
00:15:57.840 --> 00:15:59.956
You tell me where
you want to dig, Mikey,

298
00:16:00.080 --> 00:16:02.958
and I'll make goddamn sure
the bills are paid.

299
00:16:04.240 --> 00:16:05.832
No offense, Wells, but...

300
00:16:05.960 --> 00:16:08.679
you look like you had to rob
someone just to get here, man.

301
00:16:08.800 --> 00:16:11.633
You know nothing about me.

302
00:16:11.760 --> 00:16:13.955
You know it's still out there.

303
00:16:15.200 --> 00:16:17.270
I know that.

304
00:16:17.400 --> 00:16:20.278
I can see it in your eyes.
You still believe.

305
00:16:22.160 --> 00:16:25.470
I get it, and I was born on
the side of the mountain, too.

306
00:16:25.600 --> 00:16:27.216
My-my father scratched
everything he ever owned

307
00:16:27.240 --> 00:16:28.593
out of a fucking rock.
All right?

308
00:16:28.720 --> 00:16:29.976
Died with dirt
under his fingernails,

309
00:16:30.000 --> 00:16:31.672
and I intend to do the same.

310
00:16:31.840 --> 00:16:33.558
This business...

311
00:16:33.720 --> 00:16:35.312
wrote me off years ago.

312
00:16:35.440 --> 00:16:36.736
All right, maybe you're
not running the hottest

313
00:16:36.760 --> 00:16:37.976
in your career
right now either,

314
00:16:38.000 --> 00:16:39.194
so let's prove them wrong.

315
00:16:39.320 --> 00:16:40.469
You and me.

316
00:16:40.640 --> 00:16:41.856
Let's prove them all
fucking wrong.

317
00:16:41.880 --> 00:16:43.154
And you know why?

318
00:16:44.360 --> 00:16:46.669
You do know why,
because the Ring of Fire

319
00:16:46.800 --> 00:16:49.951
is right, Mike.
You called it.

320
00:16:50.080 --> 00:16:52.799
You just haven't found it yet.

321
00:16:54.840 --> 00:16:57.035
Tell me I'm crazy.

322
00:16:57.200 --> 00:16:59.555
I'll be on the next plane.

323
00:17:04.480 --> 00:17:06.596
You're crazy.

324
00:17:16.320 --> 00:17:17.799
You have a hat, Wells?

325
00:17:20.960 --> 00:17:23.349
Yeah, I got a hat.

326
00:17:23.520 --> 00:17:24.919
Why, we going somewhere?

327
00:17:25.040 --> 00:17:27.679
Up river.

328
00:17:27.800 --> 00:17:30.189
What river is that, Mike?

329
00:17:30.360 --> 00:17:31.998
You're late.

330
00:17:32.120 --> 00:17:33.917
The only one that matters.

331
00:17:59.000 --> 00:18:00.877
The Dayak have been
panning this river

332
00:18:01.000 --> 00:18:03.468
for over 5,000 years.

333
00:18:03.640 --> 00:18:06.029
That's how it got its name,

334
00:18:06.160 --> 00:18:08.196
Daya Kensana.

335
00:18:08.320 --> 00:18:09.912
What's it mean?

336
00:18:10.040 --> 00:18:11.792
"Daya" means "upstream."

337
00:18:12.800 --> 00:18:14.791
"Kensana" means "gold."

338
00:18:15.880 --> 00:18:18.269
You got to be shitting me.

339
00:18:38.440 --> 00:18:39.953
Wells.

340
00:18:40.080 --> 00:18:41.229
Wells.

341
00:18:41.360 --> 00:18:42.918
We're here.

342
00:18:43.040 --> 00:18:46.237
So, listen, the Dayak
are warrior people,

343
00:18:46.360 --> 00:18:48.874
probably best known for ngayau,

344
00:18:49.000 --> 00:18:50.592
headhunting.

345
00:18:50.720 --> 00:18:53.154
But it doesn't happen much
anymore.

346
00:18:53.280 --> 00:18:55.157
They might be thinking
we're some traders

347
00:18:55.320 --> 00:18:56.435
or someone's mother-in-law.

348
00:19:16.720 --> 00:19:17.789
How long?

349
00:19:19.080 --> 00:19:21.435
This jungle
will... will test you.

350
00:19:22.800 --> 00:19:26.839
It will hold you up, weigh you,

351
00:19:26.960 --> 00:19:28.712
and decide your worth
within an ounce.

352
00:19:28.840 --> 00:19:30.558
Do you always talk
like this, Mike?

353
00:19:30.680 --> 00:19:33.194
Like a book on tape?

354
00:19:33.320 --> 00:19:35.629
I suppose I do.

355
00:19:37.760 --> 00:19:40.513
Where are we going?

356
00:19:40.640 --> 00:19:42.312
Up.

357
00:19:55.080 --> 00:19:57.275
Take a look.

358
00:20:06.720 --> 00:20:08.312
Oh, my God.

359
00:20:12.920 --> 00:20:14.876
It's my dream.

360
00:20:15.000 --> 00:20:16.877
There have been
a few folks up here

361
00:20:17.000 --> 00:20:18.797
tapping over the years,

362
00:20:18.920 --> 00:20:21.753
but have all focused on the
foothills far to the south.

363
00:20:21.880 --> 00:20:25.236
But what interests me
is the other way.

364
00:20:25.360 --> 00:20:27.396
That small depression,

365
00:20:27.520 --> 00:20:31.229
like a giant left a footprint
walking away.

366
00:20:31.360 --> 00:20:33.874
Something happened
there, Wells.

367
00:20:34.040 --> 00:20:37.874
Something hot and angry.

368
00:20:38.000 --> 00:20:40.594
That's where we'll find
the tasty bits.

369
00:20:41.960 --> 00:20:43.473
My dream...

370
00:20:48.320 --> 00:20:50.276
...is out there.

371
00:20:50.400 --> 00:20:52.516
Damn right it is.

372
00:20:55.520 --> 00:20:57.556
Look, if it's the big boys

373
00:20:57.680 --> 00:20:59.440
that you're thinking about
investing in us...

374
00:20:59.520 --> 00:21:01.112
you know,
the Harvard endowment

375
00:21:01.280 --> 00:21:03.714
or some...
some pension fund, right?

376
00:21:03.840 --> 00:21:06.308
I am not your guy.

377
00:21:06.440 --> 00:21:08.954
But the little guys, right,

378
00:21:09.120 --> 00:21:10.712
guys that you don't even
know about,

379
00:21:10.840 --> 00:21:12.512
the, uh, the hustlers,
the scrappers,

380
00:21:12.640 --> 00:21:14.437
the make-it-happen
motherfuckers,

381
00:21:14.560 --> 00:21:16.471
those are my guys.

382
00:21:17.840 --> 00:21:20.479
How much do we need?

383
00:21:20.600 --> 00:21:24.309
Seven. Seven-fifty to start.

384
00:21:24.440 --> 00:21:26.829
Okay.

385
00:21:27.800 --> 00:21:30.360
How much do we need?

386
00:21:30.480 --> 00:21:32.550
It isn't just a lease.

387
00:21:32.680 --> 00:21:34.352
We're in lndonesia.

388
00:21:34.520 --> 00:21:38.672
That means getting a permit
and lining pockets.

389
00:21:38.800 --> 00:21:40.597
Equipment.
You don't just do this

390
00:21:40.720 --> 00:21:42.200
with a couple of shovels
and a pickax.

391
00:21:42.360 --> 00:21:43.918
I'll get the money.

392
00:21:44.040 --> 00:21:45.758
Whatever it takes.

393
00:21:55.880 --> 00:21:58.269
- What's that?
- It's a contract.

394
00:21:58.400 --> 00:21:59.879
Read it.

395
00:22:00.000 --> 00:22:01.956
Sign it.

396
00:22:17.000 --> 00:22:18.956
Keep it.

397
00:22:20.560 --> 00:22:23.199
I could feel it in my bones.

398
00:22:23.360 --> 00:22:26.079
I was selling something
that I believed in.

399
00:22:29.360 --> 00:22:30.634
20-K minimum buy-in, right,

400
00:22:30.760 --> 00:22:32.616
and the shares are fucking
flying off the shelf.

401
00:22:32.640 --> 00:22:33.880
I mean, no risk,
no gain, right?

402
00:22:33.960 --> 00:22:36.428
No, 'cause it's coming in
right now.

403
00:22:36.560 --> 00:22:38.278
No, Acosta. Michael Acosta.

404
00:22:38.400 --> 00:22:39.616
It's the largest
copper strike in history,

405
00:22:39.640 --> 00:22:41.073
and that's the guy
that we got.

406
00:22:41.200 --> 00:22:42.976
Kay, get those shoes shined
for dancing, baby.

407
00:22:43.000 --> 00:22:44.216
We don't even have
the exploration permits.

408
00:22:44.240 --> 00:22:46.595
What are they selling?

409
00:22:46.760 --> 00:22:48.239
What are we selling?

410
00:22:50.200 --> 00:22:51.997
Mikey, we're selling the story.

411
00:22:52.120 --> 00:22:54.918
And right now,
the story is you.

412
00:22:55.040 --> 00:22:57.280
Speaking of which, what the fuck
are you doing right now?

413
00:22:57.360 --> 00:22:59.920
I'm down in Kupang City
looking at coring equipment.

414
00:23:00.040 --> 00:23:02.918
Acosta's in Kupang City
looking at coring equipment.

415
00:23:03.040 --> 00:23:04.598
He's in Kupang City
as we speak.

416
00:23:04.720 --> 00:23:06.551
It-it's a fucking...

417
00:23:06.680 --> 00:23:08.416
Kupang City. We're getting
the coring equipment...

418
00:23:08.440 --> 00:23:10.112
You're magic, Mike.
You're fucking magic.

419
00:23:21.680 --> 00:23:22.715
You there, Mike?

420
00:23:24.200 --> 00:23:26.236
Two hundred
and sixty-seven thousand

421
00:23:26.360 --> 00:23:28.396
four hundred and thirty-four...

422
00:23:28.520 --> 00:23:30.272
dollars.

423
00:23:30.400 --> 00:23:32.834
That's not exactly the number
I had in mind, Wells.

424
00:23:32.960 --> 00:23:35.155
Well, look, I may be able
to borrow a little bit

425
00:23:35.280 --> 00:23:37.077
against it, but, you know...

426
00:23:37.200 --> 00:23:38.918
No, you know what?
Truth, Mike?

427
00:23:39.040 --> 00:23:40.314
Th-that's it for now.

428
00:23:40.480 --> 00:23:43.233
Th-that's it.
Can you make it work? Hmm?

429
00:23:44.960 --> 00:23:46.154
I can make anything work.

430
00:23:46.280 --> 00:23:49.033
You work out of a bar, Wells?

431
00:23:50.320 --> 00:23:52.515
Keeping a lid on expenses,
Mike.

432
00:23:52.640 --> 00:23:54.790
Making the dollar holler.

433
00:23:56.920 --> 00:23:58.478
We're in!

434
00:24:55.560 --> 00:24:57.949
Record player. That's classy.

435
00:24:58.080 --> 00:24:59.672
Am I paying for that, Mikey?

436
00:24:59.800 --> 00:25:01.518
Six-man crews,

437
00:25:01.640 --> 00:25:04.791
12-hour shifts
around the clock.

438
00:25:04.920 --> 00:25:07.559
Tomorrow we'll start looking
at our core samples.

439
00:25:07.680 --> 00:25:09.159
Then we'll send
the most promising

440
00:25:09.280 --> 00:25:11.748
down river to the assay lab.

441
00:25:11.880 --> 00:25:13.552
The mother lode, Mike.

442
00:25:15.040 --> 00:25:16.996
You're out there, baby.

443
00:26:00.160 --> 00:26:03.436
The man that invented
the hamburger was smart, Mike.

444
00:26:03.560 --> 00:26:06.074
The man who invented
the cheeseburger,

445
00:26:06.200 --> 00:26:07.838
now, that's the genius.

446
00:26:08.000 --> 00:26:10.719
Don't ask where
the meat came from. Hmm?

447
00:26:10.880 --> 00:26:14.919
Have you ever read
Christopher Columbus' journals?

448
00:26:15.040 --> 00:26:16.837
Of course not.

449
00:26:16.960 --> 00:26:19.599
In his petition
to the queen of Spain,

450
00:26:19.760 --> 00:26:21.478
he promised the conversion

451
00:26:21.640 --> 00:26:26.395
to her holy faith
of a great number of peoples.

452
00:26:26.560 --> 00:26:31.429
And he went on
and mentioned God 26 times.

453
00:26:31.560 --> 00:26:35.030
Gold, on the other hand,

454
00:26:35.160 --> 00:26:39.199
he mentioned 114 times.

455
00:26:39.320 --> 00:26:40.833
Smart dago.

456
00:26:40.960 --> 00:26:43.030
I've been wanting
to ask you something, Wells.

457
00:26:43.200 --> 00:26:44.679
Fire away, Mike.

458
00:26:44.800 --> 00:26:47.109
What's the meaning of
the tattoo on your arm?

459
00:26:47.240 --> 00:26:48.878
Oh, it's a bird.

460
00:26:50.440 --> 00:26:54.194
It's from a poem I read
when I was a kid.

461
00:26:54.320 --> 00:26:56.470
Bird with no feet...

462
00:26:56.600 --> 00:26:58.431
sleeps in the wind.

463
00:27:12.440 --> 00:27:14.158
Assay!

464
00:27:14.280 --> 00:27:16.350
Assay! Assay!

465
00:27:36.080 --> 00:27:37.718
Mike, Mike!

466
00:27:37.880 --> 00:27:40.872
Hey. These things, they look
worse every time we get one.

467
00:27:41.000 --> 00:27:42.736
Do you remember what
I told you about patience?

468
00:27:42.760 --> 00:27:44.696
Yeah, you-you remember
what I told you about money?

469
00:27:44.720 --> 00:27:45.869
- Yeah.
- Huh?

470
00:27:46.000 --> 00:27:47.816
- You said that it wasn't a problem.
- Well, it...

471
00:27:47.840 --> 00:27:49.398
it's a fucking problem now!

472
00:28:37.360 --> 00:28:38.429
Hey!

473
00:28:41.280 --> 00:28:42.280
Hey!

474
00:28:42.400 --> 00:28:43.674
Hey...

475
00:28:43.800 --> 00:28:44.676
Hey, wait!

476
00:28:44.800 --> 00:28:45.800
Stay! Stay! Stay! Stay!

477
00:28:49.440 --> 00:28:50.440
Hey!

478
00:28:50.480 --> 00:28:51.833
Stay...

479
00:28:53.840 --> 00:28:55.717
Hey! Stay.

480
00:28:55.840 --> 00:28:57.114
Stay!

481
00:29:05.560 --> 00:29:07.551
On top of everything else,

482
00:29:07.720 --> 00:29:10.996
I... I got a bad case
of malaria,

483
00:29:11.120 --> 00:29:13.554
which kills like something
like a million people a year,

484
00:29:13.680 --> 00:29:15.079
something like that.

485
00:29:15.200 --> 00:29:18.272
Suddenly, I was on the list.

486
00:29:27.320 --> 00:29:29.788
Maybe... maybe we were
pushing them too hard.

487
00:29:29.920 --> 00:29:32.912
Maybe we should just
take it easy, you know?

488
00:29:33.080 --> 00:29:34.513
Cut them in.

489
00:29:34.640 --> 00:29:36.995
You know,
give them some ownership.

490
00:29:37.120 --> 00:29:41.477
Maybe they'll come back,
you know?

491
00:29:41.600 --> 00:29:43.591
Let them know that they
got something at the end,

492
00:29:43.760 --> 00:29:45.432
you know,
when it all works out.

493
00:29:48.800 --> 00:29:51.109
Give them a piece of the pie,
you know?

494
00:29:51.240 --> 00:29:52.309
Get up.

495
00:29:52.440 --> 00:29:54.670
Come on, get up.

496
00:29:59.240 --> 00:30:00.719
I'm freezing, Mike.

497
00:30:14.480 --> 00:30:16.710
Don't let me die
and have nothing, okay?

498
00:30:19.560 --> 00:30:21.118
Get some rest now.

499
00:31:54.920 --> 00:31:56.592
I'm pulling the plug.

500
00:31:59.600 --> 00:32:01.318
No.

501
00:32:02.640 --> 00:32:04.278
No, no, no, no, no.

502
00:32:04.440 --> 00:32:06.271
Don't pull the plug, Mike.

503
00:32:07.800 --> 00:32:10.075
No, no.
You can't do that, Mikey.

504
00:32:10.200 --> 00:32:13.192
Let's get you
out of here, Wells.

505
00:32:13.320 --> 00:32:15.072
What's the plan, Mikey?

506
00:32:15.240 --> 00:32:16.992
Hmm?

507
00:32:17.160 --> 00:32:20.755
Now, I know you got
a plan, Mike, hmm?

508
00:32:20.880 --> 00:32:23.269
I know you got a plan.

509
00:32:25.480 --> 00:32:27.232
What's the plan, hmm?

510
00:32:29.480 --> 00:32:30.480
What's the plan?

511
00:32:33.680 --> 00:32:36.194
They need clean water.

512
00:32:36.320 --> 00:32:37.719
If we get filter systems
up here,

513
00:32:37.840 --> 00:32:39.956
I think they might come back.

514
00:32:56.000 --> 00:32:59.788
I got about $1,800
on this Visa right there.

515
00:32:59.920 --> 00:33:02.912
Close to 500
on this Diner's Club.

516
00:33:09.320 --> 00:33:11.515
Use it all, hmm?

517
00:33:11.640 --> 00:33:13.949
Use it all, Mikey.

518
00:33:47.360 --> 00:33:49.954
Damned if he didn't go

519
00:33:50.080 --> 00:33:51.593
to every single place

520
00:33:51.720 --> 00:33:54.075
that every one
of our workers lived.

521
00:33:54.200 --> 00:33:56.395
Streams that had no name.

522
00:33:56.520 --> 00:33:59.432
All feeding
the great Kensana River.

523
00:34:00.920 --> 00:34:03.150
Whoa!

524
00:34:13.560 --> 00:34:14.834
But you know what?

525
00:34:14.960 --> 00:34:16.632
The workers came back.

526
00:34:18.400 --> 00:34:22.678
I spent the next few weeks
in a malarial haze.

527
00:34:22.800 --> 00:34:24.870
Should've been dead.

528
00:34:25.040 --> 00:34:28.396
But Mike kept it all going.

529
00:35:07.320 --> 00:35:09.436
What day is it?

530
00:35:10.920 --> 00:35:12.399
No idea.

531
00:35:17.560 --> 00:35:19.516
- Month?
- August.

532
00:35:21.720 --> 00:35:23.472
You been sitting there
this whole time?

533
00:35:23.640 --> 00:35:25.596
Let's not get carried away.

534
00:35:29.760 --> 00:35:32.672
What'd the report say?
What's the news?

535
00:35:35.880 --> 00:35:37.757
We pulled 17 more cores.

536
00:35:39.680 --> 00:35:41.636
What?

537
00:35:41.760 --> 00:35:44.479
- No good?
- No good.

538
00:35:44.600 --> 00:35:45.953
Mm.

539
00:35:47.920 --> 00:35:49.638
More in the vicinity of great.

540
00:35:52.280 --> 00:35:54.396
Fuck you, Mike.
Don't mess with me, man.

541
00:35:56.840 --> 00:35:59.434
Eighth of an ounce per ton.

542
00:36:11.960 --> 00:36:13.837
Eighth... eighth of
an ounce per what?

543
00:36:13.960 --> 00:36:16.030
Eighth of an ounce per ton.

544
00:36:20.760 --> 00:36:22.751
What... what are you...
what are you saying, Mike?

545
00:36:22.880 --> 00:36:25.235
We got a strike in.

546
00:36:26.600 --> 00:36:27.953
We got a gold mine?

547
00:36:29.520 --> 00:36:31.715
- We got a gold mine?
- We got a gold mine.

548
00:36:36.680 --> 00:36:39.433
We got a gold mine, Mike!
We got a gold mine, Mike!

549
00:36:39.560 --> 00:36:41.869
- We got a gold mine.
- We got a goddamn gold mine!

550
00:36:42.000 --> 00:36:44.195
We got a gold mine!

551
00:36:44.320 --> 00:36:46.993
We got a goddamn gold mine!

552
00:36:47.120 --> 00:36:48.519
We got a goddamn...

553
00:36:48.680 --> 00:36:50.079
Gold mine!

554
00:36:50.200 --> 00:36:51.679
We got a gold mine!

555
00:36:51.800 --> 00:36:53.016
I knew it, I knew it,

556
00:36:53.040 --> 00:36:55.634
I knew it, I knew it,
I knew it.

557
00:36:57.240 --> 00:37:00.630
We got a goddamn gold mine!

558
00:37:00.760 --> 00:37:02.239
Fuck you, mosquitoes!

559
00:37:04.040 --> 00:37:06.031
That moment...

560
00:37:06.200 --> 00:37:09.556
there's no way I could possibly
describe the feeling.

561
00:37:09.680 --> 00:37:11.557
It's amazing
how a little gold dust

562
00:37:11.680 --> 00:37:13.830
can just change everything.

563
00:37:14.000 --> 00:37:17.470
Now, for better or worse,
the ride had begun,

564
00:37:17.600 --> 00:37:19.556
and what a goddamn ride.

565
00:37:22.120 --> 00:37:23.235
Okay, okay.

566
00:37:23.360 --> 00:37:24.509
Oh, thank God.

567
00:37:26.440 --> 00:37:28.749
- To the best goddamn team in the business.
- Fucking-A.

568
00:37:28.880 --> 00:37:30.040
You're a stand-up guy, Kenny.

569
00:37:30.160 --> 00:37:32.674
- Sharing the wealth.
- Crazy, huh?

570
00:37:32.800 --> 00:37:33.800
To the mother lode.

571
00:37:33.920 --> 00:37:35.592
The mother lode.

572
00:37:46.200 --> 00:37:47.758
All right, baby.
Do me a favor.

573
00:37:47.880 --> 00:37:49.313
Close your eyes, will ya?

574
00:37:49.440 --> 00:37:51.431
- All right.
- Cover them with your hands.

575
00:37:52.800 --> 00:37:54.472
- Keep 'em closed.
- Okay.

576
00:37:56.640 --> 00:37:58.949
- I got you. I got you. I got you.
- Oh. Okay.

577
00:38:01.040 --> 00:38:05.716
One, two, three.

578
00:38:11.000 --> 00:38:12.752
It's gonna be our place.

579
00:38:12.880 --> 00:38:15.553
Away from it all, above it all,
just like we always wanted.

580
00:38:15.680 --> 00:38:17.033
You like it?

581
00:38:17.160 --> 00:38:18.718
- Huh?
- Yes, of... Yes.

582
00:38:18.840 --> 00:38:22.753
Okay, look... the house...
right here, all right?

583
00:38:22.880 --> 00:38:25.519
The kitchen, facing there.
The great room over here.

584
00:38:25.640 --> 00:38:27.471
- Two fireplaces.
- No...

585
00:38:27.600 --> 00:38:28.669
Can we afford this?

586
00:38:28.800 --> 00:38:30.677
Almost, baby. Almost.
We're almost there.

587
00:38:30.800 --> 00:38:32.880
All right, look at this.
Couple bedrooms on that end.

588
00:38:32.960 --> 00:38:34.632
- Couple bedrooms on that end.
- Okay.

589
00:38:34.760 --> 00:38:36.671
Look at this playground
for the kids, huh?

590
00:38:36.800 --> 00:38:38.028
How many kids
you want to have?

591
00:38:43.960 --> 00:38:45.359
Kitchen has to stay over here.

592
00:38:45.480 --> 00:38:46.959
We gotta put it over here

593
00:38:47.080 --> 00:38:48.400
- to get the morning light.
- Yes!

594
00:38:48.440 --> 00:38:49.896
And a second kitchen
over on this side.

595
00:38:49.920 --> 00:38:52.036
A third one across the creek.

596
00:38:52.160 --> 00:38:54.037
A fourth one
up there with the elk.

597
00:39:12.400 --> 00:39:14.516
- Kenny, Kenny.
- Mm?

598
00:39:14.640 --> 00:39:15.914
This is Walt Kealer.

599
00:39:16.080 --> 00:39:17.832
- He publishes Gold Digger magazine.
- Mm.

600
00:39:18.000 --> 00:39:19.991
We reach
half a million subscribers,

601
00:39:20.120 --> 00:39:21.519
and they'd all love to know

602
00:39:21.640 --> 00:39:23.400
what you got cooking
over there in lndonesia.

603
00:39:23.480 --> 00:39:25.391
If you have a minute,
I'll buy you a drink.

604
00:39:27.320 --> 00:39:29.311
He's all yours, Walt.

605
00:39:34.000 --> 00:39:35.320
Hell, I read
Gold Digger magazine.

606
00:39:35.440 --> 00:39:37.317
Come on over.

607
00:39:37.440 --> 00:39:39.456
They say you bring this in,
you'll get the Golden Pickaxe.

608
00:39:39.480 --> 00:39:41.296
- Aw, shut up.
- What do you say about that?

609
00:39:41.320 --> 00:39:44.517
I'd say you
probably just jinxed it.

610
00:39:44.640 --> 00:39:47.677
- How many ounces you guessing?
- Hmm.

611
00:39:47.800 --> 00:39:50.633
Millions, Walt.
Absolutely millions.

612
00:39:50.760 --> 00:39:51.954
Placer mining?

613
00:39:52.080 --> 00:39:54.594
No. Hard rock.
This is varsity ball.

614
00:39:54.720 --> 00:39:56.199
How's the infrastructure?

615
00:39:56.320 --> 00:39:57.912
Building it
from the ground up.

616
00:40:01.680 --> 00:40:03.033
Any message
you'd like to give

617
00:40:03.160 --> 00:40:05.390
to the readers
of Gold Digger magazine?

618
00:40:09.880 --> 00:40:14.158
The last card you turn over
is the only one that matters.

619
00:40:14.280 --> 00:40:17.078
- Kenny, it's good to see you.
- Good morning, Beverly.

620
00:40:17.200 --> 00:40:19.111
Clive, how are you?

621
00:40:19.240 --> 00:40:21.595
- There he is.
- Good to see you, sir.

622
00:40:21.720 --> 00:40:23.676
- It's good to see you, son.
- Mm-hmm.

623
00:40:23.800 --> 00:40:26.519
You know, your father and I,

624
00:40:26.640 --> 00:40:28.790
we started together
in this business.

625
00:40:28.920 --> 00:40:31.434
Hell, I mean,
he put me in the business.

626
00:40:31.560 --> 00:40:33.198
I don't have to tell you that.

627
00:40:33.320 --> 00:40:36.676
He always spoke very highly
of you, Clive.

628
00:40:36.800 --> 00:40:38.631
He's one of the best men
I ever knew.

629
00:40:39.720 --> 00:40:40.914
- Yeah.
- Now!

630
00:40:41.080 --> 00:40:43.116
You have gone
from four to 23 cents

631
00:40:43.240 --> 00:40:45.356
on your first assay results.

632
00:40:45.480 --> 00:40:48.438
And you pulled in two more
to confirm those findings?

633
00:40:48.560 --> 00:40:50.437
Three more.

634
00:40:50.560 --> 00:40:51.675
- Three more?
- Mm-hmm.

635
00:40:51.840 --> 00:40:53.068
Well, that's fantastic.

636
00:40:53.200 --> 00:40:56.112
Well, I would like
to underwrite

637
00:40:56.240 --> 00:40:59.437
a private placement
of Washoe stock.

638
00:40:59.600 --> 00:41:01.079
And I believe that we can raise

639
00:41:01.200 --> 00:41:03.316
anywhere between
$8 million and $10 million.

640
00:41:03.480 --> 00:41:04.708
Well, capital is definitely

641
00:41:04.840 --> 00:41:06.558
at the top of our agenda
right now.

642
00:41:06.680 --> 00:41:08.079
Well, yes, it is.

643
00:41:08.200 --> 00:41:10.794
And a lot of people are going
to want to make this deal,

644
00:41:10.920 --> 00:41:12.273
but history is on our side.

645
00:41:12.400 --> 00:41:13.674
Mm-hmm.

646
00:41:13.800 --> 00:41:15.416
But I just wish you'd
brought this to me sooner.

647
00:41:15.440 --> 00:41:18.000
- I could have helped you from the get-go.
- Hey.

648
00:41:19.520 --> 00:41:21.715
- Well, here we are.
- Here we are.

649
00:41:21.840 --> 00:41:23.239
Lloyd...

650
00:41:23.360 --> 00:41:25.590
top that off for me,
will you?

651
00:41:25.720 --> 00:41:29.918
Straight black
with a spicer chaser.

652
00:41:50.360 --> 00:41:52.316
Mr. Wells, uh, coffee.

653
00:41:52.440 --> 00:41:53.759
There we are, Lloyd.
Thank you.

654
00:41:53.880 --> 00:41:55.996
Stanton, Mr. Wells has agreed

655
00:41:56.120 --> 00:41:59.829
to give us his business
on one condition.

656
00:42:01.880 --> 00:42:04.189
Lloyd, I want you

657
00:42:04.320 --> 00:42:06.390
to personally handle
my account.

658
00:42:06.520 --> 00:42:08.158
I-I need you to be

659
00:42:08.280 --> 00:42:09.793
available to me 24/7,

660
00:42:09.960 --> 00:42:14.317
365, as you will be riding
shotgun in my jockstrap.

661
00:42:14.440 --> 00:42:17.159
Absolutely. Yes. Thank you.

662
00:42:17.280 --> 00:42:18.536
Well, let's quit
standing around here

663
00:42:18.560 --> 00:42:19.993
and go make
some goddamn money, huh?

664
00:42:20.120 --> 00:42:21.314
That sounds good to me.

665
00:42:24.000 --> 00:42:25.672
Kenny? Kenny?

666
00:42:25.800 --> 00:42:27.597
Kenny, did you read
the last report?

667
00:42:29.840 --> 00:42:31.353
Kenny, did you read
the last report?

668
00:42:31.480 --> 00:42:32.799
Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah.

669
00:42:32.920 --> 00:42:35.753
No, I'm looking at them
right now. Holy shit!

670
00:42:35.880 --> 00:42:38.474
Holy shit is right,
my friend.

671
00:42:43.720 --> 00:42:45.358
No, it's unbelievable.

672
00:42:46.600 --> 00:42:47.874
Holy shit.

673
00:42:48.040 --> 00:42:49.917
Bobby, I can hear your wife

674
00:42:50.040 --> 00:42:51.268
smiling from here.

675
00:42:51.400 --> 00:42:52.879
Right back where you belong,
Kenny.

676
00:42:53.000 --> 00:42:55.389
Yeah, there's a lot of ghosts
in this building, boys.

677
00:42:55.520 --> 00:42:57.750
Oh, nice work, Todd!

678
00:42:57.880 --> 00:42:59.791
Whoo-hoo-hoo!

679
00:43:23.520 --> 00:43:24.839
All right, men.

680
00:43:24.960 --> 00:43:27.110
Now, this stock is going
to start cooking.

681
00:43:27.240 --> 00:43:28.798
Make sure you put
a little bit away.

682
00:43:28.920 --> 00:43:31.480
Sell some. Just the long way.
Just in case.

683
00:43:31.600 --> 00:43:32.874
- Especially you.
- What?

684
00:43:33.000 --> 00:43:34.200
- Put a little bit away.
- I...

685
00:43:34.240 --> 00:43:35.416
For the first time
in your life,

686
00:43:35.440 --> 00:43:36.953
think rainy day, Bobby, okay?

687
00:43:37.080 --> 00:43:38.399
- Of-of course. Of course.
- Hey.

688
00:43:38.520 --> 00:43:39.919
- What?
- Okay?

689
00:43:40.040 --> 00:43:42.315
- Okay.
- Okay.

690
00:43:53.480 --> 00:43:56.358
Oh, we're going to be
so fucking rich.

691
00:44:10.440 --> 00:44:13.238
At least look like
you're working, man.

692
00:44:18.680 --> 00:44:21.513
Yeah?

693
00:44:26.000 --> 00:44:27.479
What do you got?

694
00:44:27.640 --> 00:44:29.392
Wildcatters, basically.

695
00:44:29.560 --> 00:44:31.994
This Mike Acosta
has had some success. Copper.

696
00:44:32.120 --> 00:44:34.395
Got taken over
by Freeport Mac.

697
00:44:34.520 --> 00:44:37.353
- It's all in there.
- Yeah. I've heard of Acosta.

698
00:44:39.680 --> 00:44:41.955
- I like this.
- But I think we start

699
00:44:42.080 --> 00:44:44.355
with the good news,
which is that we are, uh,

700
00:44:44.480 --> 00:44:47.916
50% allocated and, uh,
we want to set aside, uh,

701
00:44:48.080 --> 00:44:49.832
30 of the remaining 50
for you boys...

702
00:44:49.960 --> 00:44:52.235
There's more food here.
Don't drink through lunch.

703
00:44:52.360 --> 00:44:53.560
All right?

704
00:44:53.640 --> 00:44:55.596
Or just eat something as well.

705
00:44:55.720 --> 00:44:57.438
- Hey, Kay!
- Phone!

706
00:44:57.560 --> 00:45:00.677
- What?
- Phone, please.

707
00:45:00.840 --> 00:45:02.800
- That one. Thank you.
- You want me to answer it?

708
00:45:05.080 --> 00:45:06.672
Hello.

709
00:45:06.800 --> 00:45:09.473
Uh, Washoe Mining.
How may I direct your call?

710
00:45:09.640 --> 00:45:11.949
- Hi. Uh, Kenneth Wells, please.
- Please hold.

711
00:45:12.080 --> 00:45:14.036
Kenny, you got a call!

712
00:45:16.800 --> 00:45:18.392
- Thank you, dear.
- Love you, baby.

713
00:45:18.520 --> 00:45:19.396
All right.

714
00:45:19.520 --> 00:45:20.635
Kenny Wells.

715
00:45:20.760 --> 00:45:24.469
This is Brian Woolf at
Brown, Thomas in New York.

716
00:45:24.600 --> 00:45:26.636
I'm with the minerals
and oil group.

717
00:45:26.760 --> 00:45:28.193
No shit.

718
00:45:32.760 --> 00:45:33.988
Uh-huh.

719
00:45:35.080 --> 00:45:36.559
Uh-huh.

720
00:45:38.000 --> 00:45:39.513
Well, that sounds
outstanding, Brian.

721
00:45:39.640 --> 00:45:41.073
And let me tell you something.

722
00:45:41.200 --> 00:45:42.792
I'm looking forward to it.

723
00:45:43.640 --> 00:45:44.959
All right.

724
00:45:53.640 --> 00:45:55.870
They're fucking flying me out.

725
00:45:56.000 --> 00:45:57.416
They're fucking flying me out.

726
00:45:58.440 --> 00:45:59.919
Come on!

727
00:46:02.920 --> 00:46:04.831
Yeah, the way I see it,

728
00:46:04.960 --> 00:46:08.555
what banks do... who knows?

729
00:46:08.680 --> 00:46:11.956
But what made this country
is not tight asses in suits...

730
00:46:12.120 --> 00:46:13.576
no offense...
taking the easy way down,

731
00:46:13.600 --> 00:46:15.636
but guys like me and Mike
who jump.

732
00:46:15.760 --> 00:46:17.113
What we have

733
00:46:17.240 --> 00:46:19.515
is a private placement
in play here.

734
00:46:19.640 --> 00:46:22.473
Already over 50% subscribed.

735
00:46:22.600 --> 00:46:24.636
Now, if the numbers
keep coming in good,

736
00:46:24.800 --> 00:46:27.473
which...
they're fucking going to,

737
00:46:27.600 --> 00:46:30.160
the sky is the limit
on this thing.

738
00:46:30.280 --> 00:46:31.776
You don't have to sell us.
We've done the math.

739
00:46:31.800 --> 00:46:32.994
We believe you're sitting on

740
00:46:33.120 --> 00:46:34.792
the largest gold find
of the decade.

741
00:46:36.080 --> 00:46:38.275
May I speak plainly,
Mr. Wells?

742
00:46:38.440 --> 00:46:40.749
Of course, Brian.
That's the only way to speak.

743
00:46:40.880 --> 00:46:43.155
Our clients are going to want
to see a serious plan

744
00:46:43.320 --> 00:46:46.630
to turn lab results
in a volatile region

745
00:46:46.760 --> 00:46:49.194
into an investment we can all
stake our reputations on.

746
00:46:49.320 --> 00:46:50.196
It's not going to be a problem.

747
00:46:50.320 --> 00:46:51.560
No, it's not a problem, per se,

748
00:46:51.640 --> 00:46:54.108
but the fact remains
that neither of you have taken

749
00:46:54.240 --> 00:46:55.960
a find of this magnitude
through production.

750
00:46:56.040 --> 00:46:58.156
Wait just a minute there, Brian.

751
00:46:58.280 --> 00:47:00.794
We-we found it,
we can dig it up.

752
00:47:00.960 --> 00:47:02.376
- It's not that complicated.
- Actually,

753
00:47:02.400 --> 00:47:04.376
historically, we found
it could be quite complicated,

754
00:47:04.400 --> 00:47:06.118
and we'd like the opportunity

755
00:47:06.240 --> 00:47:09.277
to help you formulate how you
present the operation side.

756
00:47:12.040 --> 00:47:13.792
Wh-what-what exactly
does that mean?

757
00:47:13.920 --> 00:47:15.433
I'd like you both to consider

758
00:47:15.600 --> 00:47:17.079
bringing on
a strategic partner.

759
00:47:17.200 --> 00:47:19.668
Oh.

760
00:47:20.960 --> 00:47:22.552
All right.

761
00:47:22.680 --> 00:47:25.399
Have any of you ever

762
00:47:25.520 --> 00:47:27.715
set one of your...
your handmade loafers

763
00:47:27.840 --> 00:47:30.912
down in the hole
in the side of a mountain?

764
00:47:31.040 --> 00:47:32.758
- No.
- No. We have.

765
00:47:32.880 --> 00:47:34.313
Washoe has history.

766
00:47:34.440 --> 00:47:36.715
We've done a fairly thorough
analysis of Washoe,

767
00:47:36.840 --> 00:47:39.832
Mr. Wells, and we feel
like you need strategic help.

768
00:47:40.000 --> 00:47:43.117
No. No way. No.
This is a new day, all right?

769
00:47:43.240 --> 00:47:45.390
And Indonesia
is goddamn different.

770
00:47:45.520 --> 00:47:47.715
We can help you get off
the side of that mountain.

771
00:47:47.880 --> 00:47:48.915
I don't need any help

772
00:47:49.040 --> 00:47:51.031
getting off the side
of the goddamn mountain.

773
00:47:51.160 --> 00:47:52.832
I can land this plane.

774
00:47:52.960 --> 00:47:54.029
I can!

775
00:47:55.320 --> 00:47:57.754
Wells came and found me.

776
00:47:57.880 --> 00:47:59.632
I was at a low point,
I have to admit.

777
00:47:59.760 --> 00:48:03.389
It happens in this business,
ups and downs.

778
00:48:03.520 --> 00:48:06.637
But he backed me
with his last cent,

779
00:48:06.760 --> 00:48:09.035
and more than that,
he risked his life,

780
00:48:09.160 --> 00:48:10.878
almost lost it, actually.

781
